                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "This review includes the 20th anniversary surprise update.\r\n\r\nSS2 was a big letdown when it was originally released. It deviates a lot from the general real world ancient settings and biomechanoid enemies. It has been declared non canon by the devs but is it a bad game ?\r\n\r\nThe main objective is to find 5 pieces of a medallion, each one in posession of a boss in vastly different worlds. You start the game in beaches and lakes, then move to swamps riddled with witches, a japanese world with ninjas and flying sumo enemies, the volcanic kleer world, a medieval world, etc. The general atmosphere and setting is of complete goofyness.\r\n\r\nThe game comes packed with start and end of level FMV cutscenes, very low quality and never remastered. There's almost 1 hour and a half of these not so funny, full of lame jokes cutscenes that are barely an excuse to fill some time and show the next level. Apparently the devs were requested by the publisher to make the game kid friendly and it shows.\r\n\r\nAnother huge change since First and Second encounter, is the massive amount of levels. The previous games had 15 and 12 levels respectively, SS2 has 42. They are much shorter of course, with most lasting between 7 and 10 minutes instead of the long neverending gauntlets. That being said, I found this game much easier with barely any encounters being deadly. The component of figuring out the best weapons, bullet conservation and tactical positioning is mostly gone, since even in serious difficulty the game is not throwing the same number of enemies as before.\r\n\r\nMost classic weapons are back and you can dual wield them, including the cannon. There's also a new alternate attack that throws grenades, incredibly useful for clearing clusters of enemies. Secrets continue the tradition of having useful items or incredibly weird situations. My favorite being finding a secret gay wedding ceremony with a lot of monsters attending it.\r\n\r\nGimmicky levels are better implemented this time though. Sam can now ride vehicles that shoot lasers and a fun hamster ball like with spikes that gibs every enemy it touches. Near the end, there's even a sequence of timed levels where you need to find the next checkpoint while dodging or killing enemies rushing at you. Another level includes a freeze meter that requires to reach shelters or fire barrels or risk losing health. All bosses also have some puzzly component, mostly gathering special ammo to hurt them or destroying life regeneration objects.\r\n\r\nIt takes some time to gain momentum, the first levels are not as fun as the later ones, but by the time you reach the mid worlds you start seeing more of the old enemies back. Technically, the game works almost perfectly. The only bad annoyance is the physics, they chose to put boxes everywhere similar to Half Life 2, and some secrets require to stack them in very precise manners. This however is not HL2 and the boxes jitter a lot, fall down in unrealistic ways and just won't cooperate.\r\n\r\nThey really tried to make innovations and bring completely new content and unfortunately it backfired in the end. Future SS will revert back to the egyptian tone. Is it the worst main Serious Sam game ? Probably. It's still a very fun game ? Also true.",
